# DYN-TORUS and OBS-02

Date: 2026-09-23

## DYN-TORUS

The same strict operator gives inequivalent phase dynamics.

- **Heat:** `z_k(t)=exp(-lambda_k t) z_k(0)`.  Modal orientations and all
  origin-free phase characters are constant, while radii decay.
- **Complex unitary/linear-wave extension:**
  `a_k(t)=exp(-i lambda_k t)a_k(0)`.  Powers are conserved and every fixed-power
  `T5` is an exact invariant torus.  The four relational phases precess with
  fixed, nonzero combinations of strict eigenvalues.
- **Real wave equation:** every doublet is an independent 2D isotropic
  oscillator.  Its angular momentum is conserved and circular initial data
  produce exact constant-radius phase rotors.

All three calculi are block-diagonal in the strict spectral decomposition.
Cross-mode influence is exactly zero.  The internal torus therefore carries
no intrinsic adjacency/causal propagation law before a new interaction is
supplied.

HD phase persistence itself is dynamics-dependent: heat collapses fixed radii,
unitary preserves them, and wave preserves special invariant tori.  Selecting
one because it preserves `T5` would be target coding.

A one-parameter entropy-plus-gain gradient theory also cannot make all five
strict doublet radii soft simultaneously: their uniform thresholds are
approximately

`15.913, 7.609, 6.118, 5.456, 5.221`.

So exact fixed-radius `T5` is not a five-mode low-energy manifold of the
current one-control equilibrium dynamics.

## OBS-02

The generic strict phase quotient has

\[
T^5 / S^1 \cong T^4.
\]

Four primitive bispectral characters form a minimal complete coordinate set:

\[
2\phi_1-\phi_2,\quad
\phi_1+\phi_2-\phi_3,\quad
\phi_1+\phi_3-\phi_4,\quad
\phi_1+\phi_4-\phi_5.
\]

The relation lattice is primitive, so these recover the quotient globally on
the nonzero-modal stratum, modulo natural `2pi` periods.  Four angular records
are also the dimension lower bound.

Power-only records lose all four relative phases.  Full label records recover
all Fourier coefficients.  Standard coset coarse records `j mod d` preserve:

- `d=2,3,4`: zero origin-free continuous phases;
- `d=6`: at most one;
- `d=12`: all four.

A phase direction is therefore gauge **only relative to a declared observation
algebra**.
